// Copyright 2026 Particular LLC. MOHIO(TM) is a trademark of Particular LLC.
// walk -- stop at every stage of a map and look at what is there.
// Run it:  mio run cookbook/walk-inspect-a-map.mho   (no database, no setup)
//
// `flow` runs a map and says nothing. `walk` is the opposite number: it stops at
// EVERY stage, binds what is sitting there, and runs whatever the body says.
//
//   walk <map>[.<chain>]
//       at each stage: report      emit the stage
//       at each stage: log         record it
//       at each stage: <statement> run it, with `stage` bound
//   walk: done
//
// At each stage the body can read:
//   name / qualifier / path   where this stage is
//   kind                      `place` or `transform`
//   position                  where it came in the order, counting from 1
//   arrives / departs         the hop direction into and out of this stage
//   value                     what is actually sitting there, in transit
//
// A TRANSFORM STAGE SHOWS AN EMPTY VALUE, and that is not a fault. A transform is a
// step, not a place: nothing rests there. The value it produced shows up at the next
// place along, which is exactly what the report below shows.
//
// `walk` INSPECTS; it does not drive. Walking a chain nothing has flowed through
// shows the stages with nothing in them, which is a perfectly good question to ask.

shape tidy
    value as text pad.left to 6 with "0"
shape: done

map Paydata
    data
        order.total -> tidy -> ledger.amount
map: done

create order
    total "42"
create: done

create ledger
    amount ""
create: done

flow Paydata.order

// Every stage, in order, with what is sitting at each.
walk Paydata.order
    at each stage: report
walk: done

// The two SEMANTIC ends, found by the arrows rather than by counting. `first` is
// where data enters, `last` is where it comes to rest -- the pair to compare when
// asking whether a value survived the trip.
grab first stage from Paydata.order as entry
grab last stage from Paydata.order as exit
show ("entered at: " & entry.name)
show ("came to rest at: " & exit.name)
show ("carrying: " & exit.value)
